nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186512893 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512915 "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186512927 "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}