begin{bmatrix}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532646 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532648 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=2Ax+b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2Ax+b)=2A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(2A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$